<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>From BMR to TDEE: the default example</h2>
    <p>The calculator starts with a 32-year-old woman, 5 ft 6 in and 155 lb. The Mifflin-St Jeor equation estimates her basal metabolic rate (BMR), the energy her body uses at complete rest, at about 1,430 calories a day. Total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) multiplies that by an activity factor:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Activity level</th><th class="num">Factor</th><th class="num">Estimated TDEE</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Sedentary</td><td class="num">1.2</td><td class="num">1,716</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Light (1–3 days of exercise)</td><td class="num">1.375</td><td class="num">1,966</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Moderate (3–5 days)</td><td class="num">1.55</td><td class="num">2,216</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Active (6–7 days)</td><td class="num">1.725</td><td class="num">2,466</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Very active</td><td class="num">1.9</td><td class="num">2,717</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Each step up the activity scale adds about 250 calories a day. That is the single biggest uncertainty in the estimate: choosing "moderate" when "light" fits better shifts the answer as much as a deliberate change in eating would. When in doubt, pick the lower level. Most people overestimate how active they are, and daily movement outside of workouts (walking, standing, chores) varies a lot from person to person.</p>

    <h2>Why Mifflin-St Jeor</h2>
    <p>Several equations estimate BMR. Mifflin-St Jeor, published in 1990, has been found in reviews to be among the most accurate for healthy adults, which is why it is the common default. Even so, it is an average: for an individual, measured resting energy use can differ from the prediction by 10% or more. The equation also doesn't know your body composition, so 2 people with the same height and weight but different muscle mass get the same answer.</p>

    <h2>Turn the estimate into your real number</h2>
    <p>Treat the result as a starting point and let your own data correct it:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Eat close to the estimated TDEE consistently for 2 to 4 weeks, keeping a reasonably accurate log.</li>
      <li>Weigh yourself under the same conditions several times a week and look at the weekly average, not daily readings. Day-to-day swings of a few pounds from water, salt, and digestion are normal.</li>
      <li>If the average holds steady, your intake is close to your true maintenance. If it drifts, adjust intake in small steps and watch the trend again.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why weight change slows over time</h2>
    <p>The old "3,500 calories per pound" rule predicts steady loss from a fixed deficit, but real bodies adapt. As weight changes, BMR changes with it (rerun the calculator at a new weight to see this), and the body becomes somewhat more efficient. Research models of weight change, such as those from the U.S. National Institutes of Health, show progress slowing over months rather than continuing in a straight line.</p>
    <p>Calorie needs are also not the whole picture. The protein figure shown (1.6 g per kg of body weight) is a common target for active adults, but pregnancy, adolescence, diabetes, kidney disease, a history of disordered eating, and other medical conditions call for individual guidance from a clinician or registered dietitian.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">TDEE questions</h2>
    <details><summary>What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?</summary><p>BMR is the energy your body uses at complete rest. TDEE adds the energy used by daily movement, exercise, and digesting food, and is estimated by multiplying BMR by an activity factor.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How accurate are calorie calculators?</summary><p>They give a reasonable starting estimate, but individual needs can differ by 10% or more. Tracking intake and weight for 2 to 4 weeks shows your actual maintenance level.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which activity level should I choose?</summary><p>Pick the level that matches a typical week, and choose the lower option if you are unsure. Each step adds roughly 250 calories a day for an average adult.</p></details>
  </section>
